{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== THE PUSH BLOCKER IS CLEARED; now fighting ATTESTATION STALENESS (mostly self-inflicted) ===\n\nFULL SUITE ON THE e6bd TREE: 8237 passed, 74 skipped, 0 FAILED, exit 0 (43m15s).\nThe one FAILED test in the previous handoff -- test_structured_run_seam.py::\ntest_runner_regains_real_headroom_under_the_cap -- IS NOW GREEN. runner.py 782 -> 699.\nmake lint exit 0, make typecheck exit 0 (405 files).\nSo the code side of the push blocker is DONE. What is now in the way is purely gate bookkeeping.\n\nSTATE OF THE WORK: e6bd's implementation is STASHED (stash@{0} \"e6bd wip (round 2)\") plus backed up\nindependently at $CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp/e6bd-SAFETY.patch (507 lines) and e6bd-test-SAFETY.py. The tree\nis CLEAN on purpose -- see the clean-tree rule below. Do not lose the stash; there is also an\nunrelated stash@{1} from another session (\"plan-review-fastfail-notclaimable\") -- LEAVE IT.\n\n=== THREE ATTESTATION TRAPS. I hit all three. Read this before touching any gate. ===\n\n1. `set-file-impact` IS A MATERIAL EDIT AND DESTROYS THE ATTESTATION.\n   I ran it on e6bd/8229/a49e/5e75 to clear a plan-review ADVISORY (\"undeclared file impact\").\n   That instantly flipped e6bd and a49e from certified to stale-material -- i.e. clearing the\n   advisory destroyed the very attestation the advisory was attached to. Cost: two re-reviews.\n   Material = description / AC / file_impact / children. COMMENTS ARE NOT MATERIAL and are always\n   safe to write, which is why this session log can be updated freely mid-gate.\n\n2. RUN PLAN REVIEWS AGAINST A CLEAN WORKTREE.\n   I ran 7f86's review with e6bd's implementation sitting uncommitted in the tree. The result\n   PASSED and signed, then immediately read `stale-code`, and stashing/unstashing did NOT change\n   the verdict either way -- which is what told me my model was wrong.\n   MECHANISM, read out of compute_validity() in src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py:\n       deps = manifest_deps(auth_manifest)\n       base = _hash_basis(repo_root, at_current_gate_ref=True)\n       drifted = [p for p,d in deps.items() if _hash_file(p, base=base) != d]\n   The gate re-hashes each dependency file AT THE CURRENT GATE REF and compares to the digest the\n   manifest recorded at review time. There is an explicit comment that it deliberately does NOT\n   re-hash at the signature's own pinned SHA, because that tautology once made scoped drift\n   undetectable (ticket 72d9). So a review whose manifest was built from a different basis than the\n   gate reads back is born stale and NO amount of worktree shuffling fixes it. Review clean.\n\n3. AN UNSCOPED ATTESTATION GOES STALE ON EVERY COMMIT.\n   7761 is signed but UNSCOPED (no file_impact -> no deps in the manifest), so compute_validity\n   falls through to the head-drift branch and it reads stale-head at every HEAD move -- signed at\n   97791145f, HEAD already ad5ced165. With 3+ commits still to come in this stack that is 3+ wasted\n   re-reviews. Giving 7761 a file_impact (sizing.py + the two batch runners) would scope it and make\n   it survive unrelated commits. I did NOT do it: 7761 is in_progress and I do not hold it, and\n   editing material on an unclaimed ticket is the same discipline breach as working one. Do it when\n   it is next claimed -- it is free then, since the attestation is already dead.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR PARALLELISM, measured, so nobody wastes LLM spend: re-reviewing anything right now\nis throwaway work. 343b is scoped to providers.py + runner.py, and e6bd's commit rewrites runner.py.\n7761 is unscoped and dies on any commit. 8229 and a49e are BLOCKED so review-plan fast-fails with no\nLLM at all. 5e75 is unsigned and its plan depends on what 8229 actually does. Land e6bd first, THEN\nbatch the re-reviews at the new HEAD.\n\n=== PLAN REVIEWS THIS ROUND ===\n\n7f86 PASSED on the 4th attempt (3 BLOCK rounds, each a REAL defect, none cosmetic):\n  R1 (3 findings, one root): AC2 demanded runner.py <500 / run() <120, unreachable by its three\n     children (~620/~250). ADR 0056 names FOUR extractions; 7f86 covered three. Fixed AC2 to the\n     achievable bound; filed the remainder as 5e75 (LINK, not child, so it cannot gate 7f86's close).\n  R2: the _NoBuildProviderSession catch (see below).\n  R3: the leaf-invariant catch (see below).\n\ne6bd BLOCKED on a REAL scope error and is being re-reviewed. Its Scope said the spine was\nrunner.py:507-581. WRONG AT BOTH ENDS: 507 is the _call_label observability setup that sits BEFORE\nthe `try:`, and 581 stops inside the middle arm, omitting the ENTIRE broad `except Exception` arm\n(582-629) -- the arm that carries the load-bearing sampling-rejection ordering. Corrected to\n555-629 (measured at ad5ced165). The IMPLEMENTATION was always built against the right region; only\nthe plan text was wrong.\n\nTWO DEFECTS THE REVIEWS CAUGHT THAT WOULD HAVE COST REAL REWORK -- both verified by me in source\nbefore I accepted them:\n\n  a49e: tests/interfaces/store/test_execution_mode_dispatch.py:111 stubs _NoBuildProviderSession with\n  __init__/supports/is_resolvable/close and NO __enter__/__exit__, installed by\n  monkeypatch.setattr(runner_mod,\"ProviderSession\",...) at :124. The moment a49e writes\n  `with ProviderSession(cfg) as ...` that stub stops satisfying the protocol. This is NOT one of the\n  six setattr-rebind hazards -- nothing moves modules, so re-importing fixes nothing; the stub's\n  INTERFACE fell behind the call site. a49e must add __enter__/__exit__ in the same commit.\n\n  8229: build_model_settings would move `_TEMPERATURE_WITHDRAWN_LOGGED.add(resolved)` (set defined at\n  runner.py:64, mutated at :444-445) into structured_run.py -- which is a LEAF, ENFORCED by\n  test_structured_run_seam.py::test_structured_run_has_no_runtime_import_from_runner (walks the AST,\n  fails any runtime `import runner`). Two bad exits: keep the reference and the leaf test fails; move\n  the set and runner.py:64 plus the test-side .clear() alias break. REQUIRED SHAPE: build_model_settings\n  returns the dict and is PURE; the dedup .add() stays INLINE in run(). Recorded in BOTH 7f86 and\n  8229's own plan -- I edited 8229 at a moment when neither it nor 7f86 held an attestation, so the\n  edit was free; after 7f86 certifies the same edit would have cost a re-review.\n\nORDERING IS NOW A RECORDED EDGE: added `8229 blocks a49e` (e6bd blocks 8229 already existed). Note the\nside effect: a49e flipped to ready_to_work=false, so the three children are now strictly serial.\n\nPRE-EXISTING DECAY, NOT CAUSED BY e6bd: tests/unit/test_llm_failure_matrix.py holds 23 labels of the\nform \"CURRENT(runner.py:NNN)\" citing :346/:365 while the spine actually sits at 555-629 -- stale from\na layout two splits ago. They are the third tuple element (descriptions), NOT assertions, which is\nwhy the suite passes. e6bd's plan now says to retarget them to name structured_run.interpret_failure\nwith NO line number so they cannot rot a third time.\n\nGERRIT, this firing: our stack unchanged -- top 1104 (a574) +1/+1; 1014/1023/1048/1067 still V=-1\n(all already-closed tickets; the operator overrides module-size reds manually, and note I still have\nNOT been able to confirm from Gerrit that those four reds ARE module-size-only, since the vote message\nis a bare \"FAILURE:\" plus a link). FOUR NEW CHANGES from other sessions appeared and are NOT ours --\n1115 (8c35), 1116 (db1c), 1117 (5546), 1118 (deac). Leave them alone.\n\nCRON: the hourly :23 loop prompt was STALE (it named 1484 as the claimed critical path and f844 as\nclaimable; both closed and LANDED -- 1484=e1566b5f4/change 1100, f844=0a1645847/change 1102). Old job\n367d9745 deleted, replaced by 838bbb8d on the same schedule, now carrying the real critical path, the\nthree attestation traps above, and the line \"TRUST THE STORE OVER THIS PROMPT\". A firing carrying the\nOLD text may still arrive once; ignore its 1484 instruction.\n\nNEXT: e6bd review3 (clean tree) -> 7f86 re-review (clean tree) -> claim e6bd -> pop stash -> commit\n-> push -> CI -> close. Then 8229 (review only AFTER e6bd closes; it fast-fails while blocked)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785519221245870001,"uuid":"62c36409-8052-4fe7-a074-8e30d606025d"}